// The mark as pixels, for the readers of a page that cannot read a drawing.
//
// ./logo holds the artwork itself, as the paths of an SVG, and for a browser
// that is the whole answer: one drawing, drawn at whatever size the tab it
// lands in happens to be, and never a jagged edge at any of them. It is the
// better format by every measure a person looking at a screen would name.
//
// It is also a format Google Search does not read. What its index accepts for
// the little icon beside a result is BMP, GIF, ICO, PNG, JPEG, PPM or TIFF —
// a list with no SVG on it — so a site that offers nothing else is a site
// whose results come out wearing the generic globe. That is the whole reason
// this file exists: not because a raster is better, but because the one
// reader that matters most for being found cannot see the other kind.
//
// So the same drawing is carried a second time, already rasterised, in the
// three shapes the places that ask for one actually want:
//
//   png   192x192, transparent. What Google reads, and what Android uses for
//         a shortcut. Four times the 48px Google asks as a floor, so it stays
//         sharp on the surfaces that draw it big.
//   ico   16 and 32, in one file. The icon a browser fetches from
//         /favicon.ico without being told to, and the format Google's own
//         documentation writes its example in.
//   mark  256x256, opaque, with a little air around the bee. This one does
//         two jobs, which is why it is neither of the sizes either job would
//         have picked on its own. It is the iOS home-screen icon, and iOS
//         composites a transparent one onto black — a black square with a
//         black-outlined bee somewhere inside it — so it brings its own
//         paper. And it is the picture a pasted link unfurls into, where the
//         paper matters for the same reason and 256 clears the 200px floor
//         that Facebook, LinkedIn and Slack all draw the line at. An SVG is
//         no more use there than it is to Google; this is the same bug one
//         meta tag over, and the same fix.
//
// Every one of them is a palette PNG rather than a truecolour one, which is
// no loss at all on artwork of four flat colours and about a fifth of the
// bytes — and bytes here are source text a person has to scroll past, not
// just a file on a disk.
//
// They are held as base64 because a Hive source file is text and a PNG is
// not, and text is the only form a program that ships as one executable can
// carry a picture in. `hive.crypto.base64Decode` turns it back at the moment
// it is asked for; a Str holds bytes rather than validated text, which is
// what makes a decoded PNG survive being handed to an HTTP response
// untouched.
import hive.crypto

// The three, decoded. Each answers with a Result because base64Decode does,
// and the honest thing to do with a decode that cannot fail is to say out
// loud that it could: the text below is a constant, so an Error here is a
// broken build rather than a bad request, and main.hive is where that
// distinction gets acted on.
func png(): Result<Str, hive.crypto.CryptoError> {
	return decode(pngBase64())
}

func ico(): Result<Str, hive.crypto.CryptoError> {
	return decode(icoBase64())
}

func mark(): Result<Str, hive.crypto.CryptoError> {
	return decode(markBase64())
}

// What each of them is, for the Content-Type. `image/x-icon` is not the
// registered name for an ICO — that is `image/vnd.microsoft.icon` — but it is
// the one every browser has understood for twenty-five years, and the
// registered one is younger than most of the code that reads it.
func pngType(): Str {
	return "image/png"
}

func icoType(): Str {
	return "image/x-icon"
}

// base64, unwrapped, and then decoded.
//
// The three blocks below are folded at 76 columns, the way a PEM block is,
// because the longest of them is 8,692 characters and a source file with an
// 8,692-character line in it is a source file no editor is pleasant in. Go's
// decoder rejects a newline rather than skipping it, so the folds come back
// out here before it ever sees them. Nothing takes a carriage return out,
// because nothing should ever put one in: the .gitattributes beside this file
// is what settles that, and it settles it for every checkout at once rather
// than one string operation per request forever.
func decode(wrapped: Str): Result<Str, hive.crypto.CryptoError> {
	return hive.crypto.base64Decode(join(split(wrapped, "\n"), ""))
}

